sections.body:
To change an element's position, keep its position in a variable and update its CSS when it changes. If the element is `position: absolute` you can use `style.left`; `transform` works on any element:

```js
let x = 0;
function move(dx) {
  x = Math.max(0, Math.min(260, x + dx)); // keep it between 0 and 260
  rocket.style.left = x + "px";
  // or: rocket.style.transform = `translateX(${x}px)`;
}
```

`Math.min(260, ...)` turns a number bigger than 260 into 260, and `Math.max(0, ...)` turns one smaller than zero into 0. That way the rocket can't escape the field. This is called **clamping**.

The arrow keys and space normally scroll the page. In a game we don't want that: call `e.preventDefault()` only for **the keys you use**.

If the user is typing into a box, shortcuts shouldn't work. `e.target` tells you which element the event happened in:

```js
document.addEventListener("keydown", (e) => {
  if (e.target.tagName === "INPUT") return; // typing into a box
  if (e.key === "ArrowLeft" || e.key === "ArrowRight") {
    e.preventDefault(); // don't scroll the page
  }
});
```

The browser can make sounds by itself! `AudioContext` is like a small sound studio, and an `oscillator` is like a speaker that makes a tone:

```js
function beep(freq = 440) {
  try {
    const audio = new AudioContext();
    const osc = audio.createOscillator();
    osc.frequency.value = freq; // the higher, the higher the pitch
    osc.connect(audio.destination);
    osc.start();
    osc.stop(audio.currentTime + 0.15); // play for 0.15 seconds
  } catch (err) {
    console.log("Couldn't play the sound.");
  }
}
```

`try { ... } catch (err) { ... }`: if the code inside fails, the program doesn't stop; the `catch` part runs (we'll see this in detail on Day 24).

Browsers only play sound after the user has clicked somewhere or pressed a key. Sound may not work on every device, so the rest of the game **shouldn't depend** on it.

